                                                                      Concept 1: Users and license types
What a user is
A user is a person who has a login to Workfront. Every designer, developer, manager and approver in a company is a user, and each one has a profile with their name, email, job title and so on.
Workfront also remembers who did what. Every task, comment, approval and logged hour is tied to a user.

                                                                       license types
License	What the person can do	Example person
Plan (called Standard in newer Adobe documentation)	---    Create and manage projects and plan the work. The most powerful type.	Project manager 
Work	--- Do assigned tasks, update status, log hours. Cannot plan projects.	Designer, developer
Review -------	See things and add comments or approvals. Cannot do project work.	Stakeholder who approves designs
Request ------	Submit requests and track their own requests only.	Sales person asking marketing for a page


A license type decides what kind of work a user can do in Workfront. Plan users can create and manage projects, Work users do assigned tasks and log time, Review users comment and approve, and Request users submit requests.

Why this matters
Cost. Companies pay for each license, and Plan licenses cost more. They give them only to people who need to plan.
Safety. A requester can’t accidentally change a project.
Your job. When you build forms, request queues and automations, you must know who can do what. For example, if a form is meant for requesters, a person with only a Review license will not be able to use it.


                                                                     Access Level

An access level is a set of rules assigned to a user by an admin. For each type of object (project, task, issue, document, report and so on), it lists which actions the user is allowed to do: view, create, edit, delete and so on.
It is about the user’s capabilities, and it applies to all objects of that type.

Example: “This user’s access level lets them edit projects, but not delete them.”


